// @run
// @log 7
// @log 4
// @log 2
// The DESTINATION annotations are the cells under test, so their redundancy hints are
// declared rather than removed — deleting one is a different program with no destination
// to pin from.
// @hint redundant type annotation: `c` is inferred as `{r: i32}[][][]`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `c4` is inferred as `{r: i32}[][][][]`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `cb` is inferred as `{r: i32}[][]`
// Regression (silent-class-inventory D243), and the three cells are the three ways the
// binding can sit more than ONE list level below its annotated destination. Every one is a
// generated cell of `scripts/silent-sweep/d243/genbox.py --block P` rather than a
// hand-trimmed witness — cells `p01102`, `p01194` and `p01154`, all `check-clean invalid
// wasm` on `322c07f2`.
//
// `dstPinPushAnn`'s element arm iterated `av.arrElems` ONCE and asked `dstPinSrcIs` of each,
// so it saw exactly one level. For `[[iv]]` the single element is the ArrayLit `[iv]`, which
// is not the binding: the loop found nothing, NO destination was recorded, and the pin never
// ran. The predicate was already right — `pinArmListName` answers for the element spelling
// `Circle[][]` — so the cut was the SCAN's depth, which is why D184's one-cut fix (the same
// program with one fewer `[]`, and it RUNS on the merge base) could not reach it.
//
// CELL 1 is D243's own filed witness at depth 3. CELL 2 is depth 4, which no grid before
// block P generated at all — a bound at 3 cannot tell "fixed" from "fixed one level further
// out". CELL 3 is the OTHER way down a level: an un-annotated intermediate BINDING rather
// than a nested literal. `dstPinLocalDest`'s `letType >= 0` guard already keeps such a
// binding from recording a "" DISAGREEMENT, so before this it contributed no destination
// either and the binding it links had none at all — the same "an un-annotated binding is a
// LINK in the chain, not the end of it" rule the whole-value alias walk has always applied,
// asked one container out.
//
// THE LAYOUT TWIN IS DECLARED so the failure is the silent one: with `Dot` in the program the
// un-pinned literal resolves onto `Dot`'s row while the annotated destination declares the
// arm's, and the module the engine refuses says `expected (ref null $type), found
// (ref $type)` at `vl check` rc 0. Cell 3 needs it for a second reason — without a struct row
// the module-global cell is D244's coordinate instead, and the two roots would not be
// separated.
type Circle = { r: i32 }
type Sq = { s: i32 }
type Shape = Circle | Sq
type Dot = { r: i32 }

const iv = [{ r: 7 }]
const c: Circle[][][] = [[iv]]
if (((c)[0])[0])[0].r == 7 { print(7) } else { print(0) }

const iv4 = [{ r: 4 }]
const c4: Circle[][][][] = [[[iv4]]]
if ((((c4)[0])[0])[0])[0].r == 4 { print(4) } else { print(0) }

const bv = { r: 2 }
const mid = [bv]
const cb: Circle[][] = [mid]
if ((cb)[0])[0].r == 2 { print(2) } else { print(0) }
